INT. ABANDONED OPERA HOUSE - STAGE - NIGHT

Bare boards, a ghost light burning on a pole, red velvet rows rising into dark. The painted proscenium arch has cracked and flaked. Rain taps in the rafters like a distant orchestra tuning.

SABINE DELACROIX-NOLAN, 30s, in a long black coat, steps into the pool of the ghost light, a briefcase swinging from one hand. Her eyes sweep the empty seats. She is looking for someone.

From the shadows of the wings, a voice, mild and amused.

EMERIC VASSEUR (O.S.)
You came alone.

SABINE
You told me to.

EMERIC steps into the light. 50s, white dinner jacket, bow tie undone, a face of soft, sad charm. A cut on his lip. He holds a violin case in both arms like a child.

EMERIC
That was the first mistake.

SABINE
Yours or mine?

EMERIC
I haven't decided.

He walks the length of the stage, slowly, boot heels echoing in the empty hall. Stops beside her. Close enough to touch. Does not.

SABINE
Emeric.

EMERIC
Sabine.

SABINE
Where is it?

EMERIC
Where is what?

SABINE
Don't. Not tonight.

He smiles. Lays the violin case at her feet, flicks the clasps, and opens the lid.

Inside, on a bed of velvet, nested like a jewel: a pistol. And a note, folded once.

SABINE (CONT'D)
(not touching either)
What is that?

EMERIC
A choice.

SABINE
I don't want a choice.

EMERIC
Everyone wants a choice. It is merely a matter of whether they would rather make it or be made by it.

SABINE
You're quoting your father again.

EMERIC
My father was an idiot, Sabine. He was also right.

She bends. Picks up the note. Reads. Her shoulders go slack, then rigid, then slack again. She looks up, and for the first time her composure fractures, a trembling at the mouth.

SABINE
This is her handwriting.

EMERIC
Yes.

SABINE
When did she write it?

EMERIC
Eleven days ago.

SABINE
You knew.

EMERIC
I was asked not to say.

She folds the note. Closes her fist around it. Her breath catches and then steadies.

SABINE
Is she alive?

EMERIC
I don't know.

SABINE
Emeric.

EMERIC
I honestly do not know.

The ghost light flickers. The rain thickens in the rafters. Sabine lifts her eyes to the dark upper galleries, then to the pistol.

SABINE
Then I choose.

EMERIC
Which?

She takes the gun.
